    <body>
      <sec id="article-32017.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Keloids are benign raised scars that develop after cutaneous injury due to abnormal wound healing and can cause cosmetic and functional concerns. Although normal healing results in a flat scar, some individuals develop dysregulated repair with excessive collagen deposition and tissue proliferation. These lesions may appear months to years after injury and can continue to enlarge over time. This activity provides an overview of the pathophysiology of keloid formation and highlights key clinical features and evaluation strategies. It also reviews the strengths and limitations of current treatment modalities, including the role of radiation therapy. Emphasis is placed on timely recognition and appropriate management to reduce recurrence and improve outcomes. The role of the interprofessional team is also discussed, with an emphasis on coordinated care and patient education. Participation equips clinicians with the knowledge and skills needed for effective collaborative management.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the cellular and molecular mechanisms underlying keloid formation.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Compare keloid recurrence rates after steroid injections, surgical excision alone, and surgical excision followed by radiation therapy.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Determine the optimal post-surgical radiation therapy plan for keloid management.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply effective communication strategies to coordinate interprofessional care and optimize outcomes for patients undergoing keloid treatment.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=32017&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=32017">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Keloids are benign scars that result from excessive tissue proliferation and collagen deposition during wound healing. The two most distinct phenotypes are superficial spreading (flat) and bulging (raised) keloids (see <bold>Images</bold>. Keloid Scars, Keloid Scars 01, Earlobe Keloids).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r1">[1]</xref> Although benign, keloids can negatively affect patient self-esteem and quality of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r2">[2]</xref> Under normal circumstances, healing of a superficial cutaneous injury results in a flat scar; however, dysregulation of fibroblast signaling during wound healing can lead to persistent growth of a painful, pruritic keloid. Keloids may appear months to years after the initial cutaneous insult and can continue to enlarge without spontaneous regression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The etiology of keloid formation is not fully understood. Current evidence suggests a multifactorial pathogenesis involving genetic predisposition, dysregulated immune responses, and abnormal wound-healing processes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Although some keloids arise spontaneously, most develop after pathologic wound healing following trauma or surgery and may appear years after the initial injury.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;Inciting events include acne, body piercings, tattoos, insect bites, vaccinations, surgery, and mechanical trauma. Keloids most commonly occur in areas of increased skin tension, such as the sternum and earlobes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>The prevalence of keloids varies by region and race; it is higher in individuals with darker skin types, including Black, Hispanic, and Asian individuals, as well as those with a family history of keloids. Keloid formation is most common in patients aged 10 to 30 and during hormonal changes such as puberty or pregnancy; additional factors include blood type A and hyper-IgE syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r6">[6]</xref> A large epidemiologic study in an Asian population found that patients with keloids had a 1.49-fold increased risk of cancer, particularly skin and pancreatic cancers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The physiologic response to cutaneous injury is scar formation, a process involving inflammation, proliferation, and remodeling (maturation) of the extracellular matrix (ECM). Keloids arise from pathologic wound healing and excessive dermal fibrosis. Prolonged inflammation can disrupt the balance between ECM deposition and degradation, leading to the accumulation of collagen and other components. This overabundance in the dermis and subcutaneous tissue results in excessive scarring and keloid formation.</p>
        <p>Both genetic and environmental factors influence keloid pathophysiology. Fibroblasts, the primary producers of collagen and ECM, are central to the fibrotic process. Other cellular contributors include myofibroblasts, keratinocytes, melanocytes, and mast cells. Regulatory molecules, including transforming growth factor beta, the Janus kinase/signal transducer and activator of transcription pathway, platelet-derived growth factor, vascular endothelial growth factor, and proteolytic enzymes, have all been shown to play important roles in the sequence of events leading to keloid formation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Histologically, keloids are characterized by abundant, tightly packed but disorganized collagen (see <bold>Image</bold>. ILKkeloid). The epidermis is often normal in appearance, while the dermis contains hypocellular collagen bundles composed of large, thick, wavy type I and type II hyalinized fibers. Hypertrophic scars differ from keloids by having finer, more organized collagen bundles that run parallel to the epidermal surface (see <bold>Image</bold>. Hypertrophic scar). Inflammatory cell infiltration may be present in early hypertrophic scars but typically decreases as the scar matures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Keloids can occasionally appear spontaneously without preceding cutaneous trauma, but most patients have a history of surgical incisions or skin injury. A mature scar is typically flat and light-colored, whereas abnormal wound healing may result in hypertrophic scars or keloids. Hypertrophic scars are elevated, erythematous, and sometimes pruritic but remain confined to the original injury borders. Keloids share these features but extend beyond the initial injury and may invade the surrounding tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r9">[9]</xref> They can cause functional impairment, cosmetic distortion, and psychological distress.</p>
        <p>Keloids are classified as minor or major lesions. Minor keloids are small focal scars with elevations less than 0.5 cm, whereas major keloids are larger and more elevated. Both types can continue to grow, invade the surrounding tissue, and recur after excision. Keloids may occur anywhere on the body but are most common on the neck, earlobes, chest, shoulders, upper back, sternum, knees, and ankles.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r5">[5]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The most common methods for evaluating keloid scars include subjective assessment using scoring scales, such as the Vancouver Scar Scale, and objective measurement with two-dimensional photography. Less frequently used techniques include assessment of biochemical properties, ultrasonography, laser-based imaging, and biopsy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r10">[10]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Medical Treatment </bold>
</p>
        <p>No single medical therapy is universally effective for keloids. Intralesional corticosteroid injections are commonly used as first-line therapy to reduce scar volume, height, and associated symptoms such as pruritus and pain. Due to recurrence rates of 9% to 50% and frequent side effects including hypopigmentation, skin atrophy, telangiectasias, and injection pain, steroids are often combined with other treatments to improve outcomes. Other therapies with limited evidence for reducing keloid recurrence but some anecdotal success include massage, pressure garments, adhesive tape or silicone gel sheeting, cryotherapy, and laser or light treatments.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical Treatment </bold>
</p>
        <p>Surgical excision is frequently used to treat mature keloid scars but is ineffective as monotherapy, with recurrence rates of 45% to 100%. To minimize recurrence, surgery is typically combined with adjuvant treatments such as radiotherapy, cryotherapy, or topical steroids. Recommended surgical techniques include intramarginal fusiform excision with minimal tension and everted closure. Use of subcutaneous or fascial tension-reducing sutures, z-plasty, and local flap transfer may further reduce the risk of keloid recurrence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Keloids can resemble hypertrophic scars but are distinguished by continuous growth beyond the boundaries of the original injury. Dermatofibromas are another atypical hyperpigmented scarlike lesion that may mimic keloids; however, they typically exhibit a dimple sign, in which the lesion depresses centrally when lateral pressure is applied.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s11" sec-type="Radiation Oncology">
        <title>Radiation Oncology</title>
        <p>Surgical excision followed by immediate postoperative radiation therapy is the most effective treatment for keloids, with recurrence rates around 20%, compared&#x000a0;to surgery or radiation alone.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;Radiation is often indicated for recurrent keloids or patients at high risk of recurrence, including those with marginal resections, extensive lesions, or unfavorable locations. Initiating radiation on the same day as surgical removal is most effective and typically requires coordinated care between the surgeon and radiation oncologist.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Radiation Dosing and Fractionation</bold>
</p>
        <p>Radiation prescriptions vary, with total doses ranging from 12 to 20 Gy. Historically, low-dose fractionation (3-4 fractions, each 3-4 Gy) was used. Recent data suggest better long-term control with a biologically effective dose greater than 30 Gy, such as 13 Gy in 1 fraction, 16 Gy in 2 fractions, or 18 Gy in 3 fractions over 5 to 7 days.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Radiation Treatment Planning</bold>
</p>
        <p>The scar plus a 1-cm radial margin and 0.5- to 1-cm depth is targeted. Clinical or computed tomography simulation can be used depending on lesion location and nearby critical structures. Patients should be positioned reproducibly, and lead shields or absorbers can protect normal tissues. For example, earlobe lesions are simulated with the patient in a supine position, head rotated and immobilized with an Aquaplast mask. Scar and margin wiring may guide electron cutouts and treatment planning, and a lead shield with wax cover can protect the neck and brain.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Radiation Modalities</bold>
</p>
        <p>Different forms of radiation can be used to treat superficial targets, including electrons (&#x02264;6 MeV), radiography (70&#x02013;150 kV), and iridium-192 brachytherapy delivered via implants or surface applicators. Electrons are the most commonly used modality for keloids. They are generated by a standard linear accelerator&#x02014;the same machine used to deliver photon therapy in most radiation treatments. Electrons have shallow tissue penetration compared&#x000a0;to photons, making them well-suited for treating superficial skin lesions while minimizing exposure to underlying structures. A 1-cm bolus is often placed over the lesion to increase the skin dose and reduce penetration depth.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Superficial&#x000a0;radiography therapy uses photon beams to deliver energy to a maximum depth of 5 mm. Like external beam radiation therapy, it can treat superficial lesions without damaging the underlying tissue. This method is less costly and easier to use than electron-based external beam radiation therapy but may result in uneven dose distribution at the periphery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Brachytherapy with iridium-192 is an effective treatment for keloids but is less widely available because not all centers have the necessary radioactive source. A surface applicator or custom mold is typically created to deliver radiation to the excision site and surrounding tissue at risk of recurrence. Dose heterogeneity can increase the risk of skin toxicity. In a meta-analysis by Mankowski et al, brachytherapy had the lowest recurrence rate (15%) compared with external beam radiation therapy (23%) and&#x000a0;radiography therapy (23%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r16">[16]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s12" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Numerous studies have shown that surgical excision followed by radiation therapy is the most effective treatment for keloids. A literature review of over 70 studies demonstrated that postexcisional radiotherapy significantly reduced recurrence rates (22%&#x000b1;4%) compared with radiation alone (37%&#x000b1;12%). The same meta-analysis found that among radiation modalities, postexcisional brachytherapy had the lowest recurrence rate at 15%, compared with 23% for both postexcisional electron beam therapy and postexcisional radiography therapy.</p>
        <p>Keloid recurrence also varies by anatomic location. Lesions on the chest and trunk have the highest recurrence rate at 34%, whereas keloids on the ear have the lowest at 12%. Recurrence differences are thought to relate, in part, to variations in skin tensile strength across these areas.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r16">[16]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s13" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>The most common complications of radiation therapy for keloids, affecting about 30% of patients, are erythema and pigmentary changes such as hypopigmentation or hyperpigmentation. Acute complications, which may develop during treatment or within 7 to 10 days, are related to total radiation dose and can include erythema, edema, desquamation, ulceration, or necrosis. Late complications, arising weeks to months after therapy, are dose-dependent and may include pigmentary changes, alopecia, atrophy, and telangiectasia. In fewer than 1% of cases, infection or wound dehiscence may occur. Use of emollients or topical corticosteroids after radiation therapy may help reduce&#x000a0;adverse effects. A systematic review reported no instances of postsurgical keloid formation following cosmetic eyelid procedures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r17">[17]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s14" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>The risk of developing a secondary malignancy after radiation therapy for keloids is minimal in adults.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r18">[18]</xref> In a survey of 114 practicing radiation oncologists, 99.1% supported the use of radiation therapy for keloid treatment in adults, whereas only 54.9% supported its use in pediatric patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-32017.r19">[19]</xref> This suggests that radiation therapy is generally considered a safe option for adults but may not be standard first-line treatment in children.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s15" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Key pearls in the management of keloids include early recognition of high-risk patients and timely intervention to reduce recurrence. Keloids can cause significant cosmetic and functional concerns as well as psychological distress, and treatment outcomes are improved when a combination of surgical excision and adjuvant therapies, such as radiation, is employed. Common pitfalls include underestimating the propensity for regrowth, delaying adjuvant therapy, or using monotherapy, which increases recurrence risk. Prevention strategies focus on minimizing skin trauma, using meticulous surgical technique, and providing patient education regarding the risk of keloid formation after injury or procedures. Awareness of anatomical sites prone to recurrence and the limitations of each therapy can help optimize long-term outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-32017.s16" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Management of keloids is challenging and is best approached by an interprofessional team that may include a dermatologist, plastic surgeon, and radiation oncologist. Cosmetic concerns primarily drive treatment, and asymptomatic lesions without aesthetic impact often require no intervention. Various treatments are available, including surgery, corticosteroid injections, and radiation therapy. No approach consistently guarantees success, and all carry a risk of worsening the keloid. Primary care providers play a key role in educating patients about the risks, benefits, and limitations of each approach. Coordinated communication among healthcare professionals ensures that patient preferences are respected, interventions are appropriately timed, and care is patient-centered, with an emphasis on shared decision-making and realistic expectations. In many cases, observation may remain the most appropriate strategy.</p>
      </sec>
